\title{The Fundamental Group as a Complete Invariant of Homotopy 1-Types:\\
Realization, Whitehead, Sharpness, and the Role of $\pi_0$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 4, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, self-contained classification of homotopy $1$-types and of the maps
between them, using groupoids as the algebraic model. For connected $1$-types with
basepoints --- the algebraic models of Eilenberg--MacLane spaces $K(G,1)$ --- we prove a
\emph{realization theorem}: every homomorphism $\varphi:\pi_1(X)\to\pi_1(Y)$ of fundamental
groups is induced by an actual map of $1$-types. We prove that two maps out of a connected
$1$-type are homotopic if and only if a single isomorphism at the basepoint intertwines the
two induced actions of the fundamental group, and we deduce the classification bijection
$[K(G,1),K(H,1)]\cong\Hom(G,H)/\conj$, together with its functoriality. We compute the
fibres of the realization map: the homomorphisms inducing a fixed homotopy class form a
coset space of the centralizer of the image, so their number is the index
$[H:C_H(\varphi(G))]$. We prove the Whitehead theorem for $1$-types and its converse. We
then prove that the hypotheses are sharp in the strongest possible sense: the one-point
space and the discrete two-point space have isomorphic homotopy groups in \emph{every}
degree, yet are not homotopy equivalent. Finally we identify precisely the missing datum:
defining $\pi_0$ of a $1$-type as its set of isomorphism classes of objects, we prove that
every $1$-type decomposes as the disjoint union of its components, that disjoint unions of
connected $1$-types are classified by an index bijection matching fundamental groups, and
hence that the pair ($\pi_0$, the family of fundamental groups of the components) is a
complete invariant of arbitrary homotopy $1$-types.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}
The fundamental group $\pi_1(X,x)$ is the archetypal algebraic invariant of a space:
functorial, computable, and lossy. It is lossy by design --- it sees only dimension one ---
and the apparatus of higher homotopy theory exists because of what it discards. The
classification problem addressed here is the complementary one: \emph{on which class of
spaces, and for which class of maps, is the fundamental group a complete invariant, and
what exactly happens at the boundary of that class?}

The expected answer is ``aspherical spaces'', i.e.\ Eilenberg--MacLane spaces $K(G,1)$.
Making this precise involves several separate statements usually treated as folklore:
(1) connected $1$-types with isomorphic fundamental groups are homotopy equivalent;
(2) every homomorphism of fundamental groups is realized by a map;
(3) two maps agree up to homotopy exactly when the induced homomorphisms are conjugate;
(4) a $\pi_1$-isomorphism between connected $1$-types is a homotopy equivalence;
(5) none of this survives dropping connectedness, and the failure is severe;
(6) adding $\pi_0$ restores completeness for all $1$-types.
This paper proves all six and quantifies (3) by computing the fibres of realization.

\subsection{The algebraic model}
We work with \emph{groupoids}: categories in which every morphism is invertible. The
fundamental groupoid $\Pi_1(X)$ --- objects the points of $X$, morphisms the homotopy
classes rel endpoints of paths --- is the canonical example, and for a homotopy $1$-type it
is a faithful algebraic replacement for the space. The dictionary is:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{l|l}
Topology & Algebra\\\hline
homotopy $1$-type $X$ & groupoid $\CC$\\
point $x\in X$ & object $c\in\CC$\\
$\pi_1(X,x)$ & vertex group $\Aut(c)$\\
path-connected & connected groupoid\\
$\pi_0(X)$ & set of isomorphism classes of objects\\
continuous map & functor\\
homotopy of maps & natural isomorphism of functors\\
homotopy equivalence & equivalence of categories\\
disjoint union & coproduct of groupoids
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Every theorem below is stated and proved in this algebraic setting, where it is exact;
Section~\ref{sec:top} transports the main statements back to topology.

\subsection{Conventions and basic definitions}
Throughout, $\CC,\DD,\EE$ denote groupoids; composition is written left-to-right, so
$f\cdot g$ means ``first $f$, then $g$'', and $\mathbf{1}_X$ is the identity of $X$.

\begin{definition}[Vertex group]
For an object $c$ of a groupoid $\CC$, the \emph{vertex group} $\Aut(c)$ is the group of
isomorphisms $c\to c$ under composition. In a groupoid every endomorphism is invertible, so
$\Aut(c)$ coincides with the endomorphism monoid $\End(c)$; the isomorphism sends an
isomorphism to its underlying morphism, with inverse supplied by groupoid inversion.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Connectedness]
A groupoid $\CC$ is \emph{connected at $c$} if every object admits an isomorphism from $c$.
This is the algebraic form of path-connectedness with basepoint $c$; a connected groupoid
with vertex group $G$ is the algebraic model of a $K(G,1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equivalence]
An \emph{equivalence} of groupoids is a functor which is full, faithful and essentially
surjective; equivalently, one admitting a quasi-inverse. This is the algebraic form of
homotopy equivalence.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\pi_0$]
For a category $\CC$, isomorphism of objects is an equivalence relation; the quotient set
$\pi_0(\CC)=\Ob(\CC)/\!\cong$ is the set of \emph{connected components}. A functor $F$
induces a well-defined map $\pi_0(F)$, since functors preserve isomorphisms.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Conjugacy of homomorphisms]
Homomorphisms $\varphi,\psi:G\to H$ are \emph{conjugate} if there is $u\in H$ with
$\psi(a)=u\varphi(a)u^{-1}$ for all $a$. This is an equivalence relation, and the quotient
set is denoted $\Hom(G,H)/\conj$.
\end{definition}

\section{Chosen paths and the loop construction}

\begin{definition}[Chosen paths]
Let $\CC$ be connected at $c$. A \emph{base path system} assigns to each object $X$ an
isomorphism $p_X:c\to X$, normalized so that $p_c=\mathbf{1}_c$. Such a system exists:
choose $p_X$ among the nonempty set of isomorphisms $c\to X$ for $X\neq c$, and take the
identity at $X=c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Loop of a morphism]
For $g:X\to Y$ put $\ell(g)=p_X\cdot g\cdot p_Y^{-1}\in\Aut(c)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Properties of $\ell$]\label{lem:loop}
For composable $f:X\to Y$, $g:Y\to Z$, objects $X$, and $a\in\Aut(c)$:
(1) $\ell(\mathbf{1}_X)=1$; (2) $\ell(f\cdot g)=\ell(g)\cdot\ell(f)$ in $\Aut(c)$;
(3) $\ell(a)=a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(1) $p_X\cdot\mathbf{1}_X\cdot p_X^{-1}=1$. (2) Insert $p_Y^{-1}p_Y$:
$p_X f g p_Z^{-1}=(p_X f p_Y^{-1})(p_Y g p_Z^{-1})$; the product in $\Aut(c)$ is
composition in the opposite order, which accounts for the swap. (3) $p_c=\mathbf{1}_c$.
\end{proof}

Property (3) is the point of the normalization: the loop construction restricts to the
identity on the fundamental group, so the functors built from it induce exactly the
homomorphism one started with, with no correction term.

\section{Realization: every homomorphism is induced by a map}

\begin{definition}[Realization functor]
Let $\CC$ be connected at $c$ with a base path system, let $\DD$ be a groupoid with a chosen
object $d_0$, and let $\varphi:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(d_0)$ be a homomorphism. Define
$R_\varphi:\CC\to\DD$ by $R_\varphi(X)=d_0$ for all $X$ and
$R_\varphi(g)=\varphi(\ell(g))$ for all morphisms $g$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$R_\varphi$ is a functor, and $R_\varphi(a)=\varphi(a)$ for every $a\in\Aut(c)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Identities: $R_\varphi(\mathbf{1}_X)=\varphi(1)=\mathbf{1}_{d_0}$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:loop}(1). Composition:
$R_\varphi(f\cdot g)=\varphi(\ell(g)\ell(f))=\varphi(\ell(g))\varphi(\ell(f))$, which,
unwinding the group multiplication back into composition order, is $R_\varphi(f)\cdot
R_\varphi(g)$. The last claim is Lemma~\ref{lem:loop}(3).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realization]\label{thm:realize}
Let $\CC$ be a groupoid connected at $c$, let $\DD$ be a groupoid, $d_0\in\DD$, and let
$\varphi:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(d_0)$ be any group homomorphism. Then there exist a functor
$F:\CC\to\DD$ and an isomorphism $e:F(c)\to d_0$ with
\[
e^{-1}\cdot F(a)\cdot e=\varphi(a)\qquad\text{for all }a\in\Aut(c).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $F=R_\varphi$ and $e=\mathbf{1}_{d_0}$.
\end{proof}

Topologically: the map $[K(G,1),K(H,1)]\to\Hom(G,H)$ is surjective. Note how little is
required: $\DD$ need not be connected, and $\varphi$ need not be injective or surjective.

\section{Homotopies are conjugations}

\begin{definition}[Induced homomorphism]
If $\DD$ is connected at $d_0$ with base path system $q$, a functor $F:\CC\to\DD$ induces
$F_*:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(d_0)$, $F_*(a)=q_{F(c)}\cdot F(a)\cdot q_{F(c)}^{-1}$. One has
$(R_\varphi)_*=\varphi$ when $q$ is normalized at $d_0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Homotopy criterion]\label{thm:homotopy}
Let $\CC$ be connected at $c$, let $\DD$ be any category, and let $F,G:\CC\to\DD$ be
functors. Then $F$ and $G$ are naturally isomorphic if and only if there is an isomorphism
$h:F(c)\to G(c)$ with
\[
F(a)\cdot h=h\cdot G(a)\qquad\text{for all }a\in\Aut(c).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $\alpha:F\Rightarrow G$ is a natural isomorphism, set $h=\alpha_c$;
naturality applied to $a:c\to c$ is exactly the displayed identity.

($\Leftarrow$) Choose a path system $p$ and set
$\alpha_X=F(p_X)^{-1}\cdot h\cdot G(p_X):F(X)\to G(X)$. The key point is
\emph{path-independence}: for any other isomorphism $p':c\to X$, put $a=p'\cdot p_X^{-1}\in
\Aut(c)$, so $p'=a\cdot p_X$; then
\[
F(p')^{-1}h\,G(p')=F(p_X)^{-1}\big(F(a)^{-1}h\,G(a)\big)G(p_X)=F(p_X)^{-1}h\,G(p_X),
\]
using the hypothesis for $a^{-1}$. Naturality follows: for $f:X\to Y$, the composite
$p_X\cdot f$ is a legitimate choice of path to $Y$, whence
\[
F(f)\cdot\alpha_Y=F(f)F(p_X f)^{-1}h\,G(p_X f)=F(p_X)^{-1}h\,G(p_X)\cdot
G(f)=\alpha_X\cdot G(f).
\]
Each $\alpha_X$ is a composite of isomorphisms, hence invertible.
\end{proof}

Connectedness is used exactly once, but decisively: it lets a single isomorphism at the
basepoint propagate coherently over the whole $1$-type.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:intertwine}
For $p,q,u$ in a group, $p u=u q$ holds if and only if $q=u^{-1}pu$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of maps]\label{thm:conj}
Let $\CC$ be connected at $c$, $d_0\in\DD$, and $\varphi,\psi:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(d_0)$. Then
$R_\varphi\simeq R_\psi$ if and only if $\varphi$ and $\psi$ are conjugate in $\Aut(d_0)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both realizations send every object to $d_0$, so the isomorphism $h$ of
Theorem~\ref{thm:homotopy} is an element $u\in\Aut(d_0)$, and the intertwining condition
$\varphi(a)u=u\psi(a)$ for all $a$ is, by Lemma~\ref{lem:intertwine}, exactly
$\psi=u^{-1}\varphi u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The classification bijection]\label{cor:class}
Let $\CC$ be connected at $c$ and $\DD$ connected at $d_0$, with $G=\Aut(c)$,
$H=\Aut(d_0)$. Then $F\mapsto[F_*]$ and $[\varphi]\mapsto[R_\varphi]$ are mutually inverse
bijections
\[
[\CC,\DD]\;\xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }\;\Hom(G,H)/\conj .
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Both maps are well defined: homotopic functors induce conjugate homomorphisms (evaluate the
natural isomorphism at $c$ and transport along the chosen path), and conjugate
homomorphisms have homotopic realizations (Theorem~\ref{thm:conj}). One composite is the
identity since $(R_\varphi)_*=\varphi$. The other is the identity because every functor out
of a connected groupoid is naturally isomorphic to the realization of the homomorphism it
induces: the isomorphism $q_{F(c)}^{-1}$ at the basepoint intertwines the two actions, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:homotopy} gives $F\cong R_{F_*}$.
\end{proof}

Topologically this is $[X,K(H,1)]\cong\Hom(\pi_1X,H)/\conj$ for $X$ a connected $1$-type,
in its sharpest form: the bijection is exhibited by explicit mutually inverse
constructions.

\begin{theorem}[Functoriality]\label{thm:funct}
(1) $(\mathrm{id}_\CC)_*=\mathrm{id}_{\Aut(c)}$.
(2) For $F:\CC\to\DD$ and $G:\DD\to\EE$ with $\DD,\EE$ connected at $d_0,e_0$, the
homomorphism $(F\circ G)_*$ is conjugate to $G_*\circ F_*$, the conjugating element being
$u=r_{G(d_0)}\cdot G(q_{F(c)})\cdot r_{G(F(c))}^{-1}$, where $q,r$ are the path systems of
$\DD,\EE$.
(3) Hence $[(F\circ G)_*]=[G_*\circ F_*]$ as conjugacy classes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) Immediate from $p_c=\mathbf{1}_c$. (2) Both sides conjugate $G(F(a))$ by a path from
$e_0$; the two paths differ by exactly $u$, and expanding and cancelling
$q_{F(c)}q_{F(c)}^{-1}$ gives the identity. (3) is (2) read in the quotient.
\end{proof}

The residual conjugation in (2) is the usual basepoint-change indeterminacy; it disappears
precisely on conjugacy classes.

\section{Fibres of the classification: a counting theorem}

Conjugation defines an action of $H$ on $\Hom(G,H)$ by $(u\cdot\varphi)(a)=u\varphi(a)u^{-1}$;
$\psi$ lies in the orbit of $\varphi$ if and only if they are conjugate.

\begin{theorem}[Stabilizer equals centralizer]\label{thm:stab}
$\Stab_H(\varphi)=C_H(\varphi(G))=\{u\in H:\ u\varphi(a)=\varphi(a)u\ \ \forall a\in G\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$u\cdot\varphi=\varphi$ means $u\varphi(a)u^{-1}=\varphi(a)$ for all $a$, i.e.\ $u$
commutes with every element of the image.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Orbit structure]\label{cor:orbit}
$\Orb_H(\varphi)\cong H/C_H(\varphi(G))$, so
$\#\{\psi:\psi\sim\varphi\}=[H:C_H(\varphi(G))]$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Fibres of realization]\label{thm:fibre}
With $\CC$ connected at $c$, $G=\Aut(c)$, $H=\Aut(d_0)$ and $\varphi\in\Hom(G,H)$,
\[
\{\psi:\ R_\psi\simeq R_\varphi\}=\Orb_H(\varphi),\qquad\text{of cardinality }
[H:C_H(\varphi(G))].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:conj} identifies the set with the orbit; Corollary~\ref{cor:orbit} counts
it.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
If $\varphi$ is trivial the centralizer is $H$ and the fibre is a single homomorphism: the
constant map is homotopically rigid. If $\varphi$ is surjective and $Z(H)=1$ the fibre has
$|H|$ elements. If $H$ is abelian every centralizer is $H$, so
$[K(G,1),K(H,1)]\cong\Hom(G,H)$ on the nose.
\end{example}

\begin{corollary}[Counting identity]\label{cor:count}
For finite $G,H$,
\[
|\Hom(G,H)|=\sum_{[\varphi]}\big[H:C_H(\varphi(G))\big],
\]
the sum being over homotopy classes of maps $K(G,1)\to K(H,1)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The Whitehead theorem for $1$-types}

\begin{theorem}[Whitehead, one-dimensional]\label{thm:whitehead}
Let $\CC$ be connected at $c$, let $F:\CC\to\DD$ be a functor, and suppose $\DD$ is
connected at $F(c)$. If $F_c:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(F(c))$ is bijective, then $F$ is an
equivalence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Injective $\Rightarrow$ faithful.} If $F(f)=F(g)$ for $f,g:X\to Y$, then $F$ sends the
loops $\ell(f),\ell(g)$ to the same automorphism, since each is the composite of
$F(p_X)$, $F(f)$ resp.\ $F(g)$, and $F(p_Y)^{-1}$. Injectivity gives $\ell(f)=\ell(g)$, and
conjugating back by $p_X^{-1}(-)p_Y$ recovers $f=g$.

\emph{Surjective $\Rightarrow$ full.} For $f:F(X)\to F(Y)$, the morphism
$g=F(p_X)\cdot f\cdot F(p_Y)^{-1}$ is an automorphism of $F(c)$; choose $b$ with $F(b)=g$.
Then $p_X^{-1}\cdot b\cdot p_Y$ maps to $f$.

\emph{Connected target $\Rightarrow$ essentially surjective.} Every object of $\DD$ is
isomorphic to $F(c)$, which lies in the image.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Converse]\label{thm:whconv}
If $F$ is an equivalence then $F_c:\Aut(c)\to\Aut(F(c))$ is bijective for every $c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Injectivity is faithfulness. For surjectivity, fullness and faithfulness give a preimage of
any automorphism of $F(c)$, and the preimage of an isomorphism under a fully faithful
functor is an isomorphism.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:complete-connected}
Two connected $1$-types are homotopy equivalent if and only if their fundamental groups are
isomorphic; and a map between connected $1$-types is a homotopy equivalence if and only if
it induces an isomorphism on fundamental groups.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
``Only if'' is Theorem~\ref{thm:whconv}. For ``if'', realize the isomorphism by
Theorem~\ref{thm:realize} and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:whitehead}.
\end{proof}

\section{Topological consequences}\label{sec:top}

\begin{proposition}
If $X$ is path-connected and $x\in X$, then $\Pi_1(X)$ is connected at $x$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $y\in X$ choose a path $\gamma$ from $x$ to $y$; its class is a morphism $x\to y$, and
the class of the reverse path is a two-sided inverse, since $\gamma\bar\gamma$ and
$\bar\gamma\gamma$ are homotopic rel endpoints to the constant paths.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Topological realization]
Let $X,Y$ be path-connected with basepoints $x,y$ and let
$\varphi:\pi_1(X,x)\to\pi_1(Y,y)$ be any homomorphism. Then there are a functor
$F:\Pi_1(X)\to\Pi_1(Y)$ and an isomorphism $e:F(x)\to y$ such that the induced homomorphism
on $\pi_1$, transported along $e$, is exactly $\varphi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The previous proposition supplies connectedness, and $\pi_1$ is the vertex group of the
fundamental groupoid; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:realize}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
Path-connected spaces with isomorphic fundamental groups have equivalent fundamental
groupoids.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Realize the isomorphism by a functor. The induced map on vertex groups is a composite of
bijections, hence bijective, and the target is connected at the image of the basepoint;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:whitehead}.
\end{proof}

For genuine $1$-types, where the fundamental groupoid is a complete model, this says that
aspherical spaces with isomorphic fundamental groups are homotopy equivalent; in general it
is the exact residue of that statement, namely that the $1$-truncations agree.

\section{Sharpness}

\subsection{Connectedness is necessary}

\begin{definition}
For a set $\alpha$, the \emph{discrete groupoid} $\mathbf{D}(\alpha)$ has object set
$\alpha$ and only identity morphisms; all its vertex groups are trivial. It is the
algebraic model of a totally disconnected space.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Connectedness is necessary]
The groupoids $\mathbf{D}(\{*\})$ and $\mathbf{D}(\{0,1\})$ have isomorphic (trivial) vertex
groups at every basepoint; the first is connected, the second is not; and they are not
equivalent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The vertex groups are trivial, so the unique map between them is an isomorphism.
$\mathbf{D}(\{*\})$ has one object. A morphism between distinct objects of a discrete
groupoid would force them to be equal, so $\mathbf{D}(\{0,1\})$ is not connected. Finally
an equivalence induces a bijection on $\pi_0$, and $1\neq 2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{No family of homotopy groups suffices}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing of all homotopy groups]\label{thm:vanish}
Let $Z$ be totally disconnected, $z\in Z$, and let $N$ be a nonempty index type. Then the
set of based maps of the cube $[0,1]^N$ sending the boundary to $z$, modulo boundary-fixing
homotopy, has exactly one element.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The cube is connected and $f$ is continuous, so its image is a connected subset of $Z$,
hence a single point. Since the boundary is nonempty (it contains the corner with all
coordinates $0$) and is sent to $z$, that point is $z$. So any two such maps are equal as
functions, and the constant homotopy $H(t,y)=f(y)$ is a boundary-fixing homotopy between
them.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[All homotopy groups agree, yet not homotopy equivalent]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $P$ be the one-point space and $B$ the two-point discrete space. Then $\pi_N(P)\cong
\pi_N(B)$ in every degree (both trivial), yet $P$ and $B$ are not homotopy equivalent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both spaces are totally disconnected, so Theorem~\ref{thm:vanish} makes all homotopy groups
trivial, and any two one-element sets are in bijection. A homotopy equivalence between
totally disconnected spaces induces a bijection of underlying sets --- homotopies into a
totally disconnected space are constant, so the homotopy inverse is a genuine two-sided
inverse --- and there is no bijection $\{*\}\to\{0,1\}$.
\end{proof}

This defeats not merely $\pi_1$ but the whole tower $\pi_1,\pi_2,\pi_3,\dots$, and isolates
the reason: the tower is blind to $\pi_0$.

\subsection{$\pi_0$ is exactly what is missing}

\begin{proposition}
$\pi_0(\mathbf{D}(\alpha))\cong\alpha$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[$\pi_0$ is a homotopy invariant]\label{prop:pi0inv}
An equivalence $\CC\simeq\DD$ induces a bijection $\pi_0(\CC)\cong\pi_0(\DD)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The functor and its quasi-inverse induce maps on $\pi_0$, and the unit and counit
isomorphisms show that the composites act as the identity on isomorphism classes.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
If $\CC$ is connected then $\pi_0(\CC)$ has at most one element.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete $1$-types are classified by $\pi_0$]
$\mathbf{D}(\alpha)\simeq\mathbf{D}(\beta)$ if and only if $\alpha\cong\beta$ as sets.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Forward: compose the two preceding propositions. Backward: a bijection of sets induces an
isomorphism, hence an equivalence, of discrete groupoids.
\end{proof}

\section{The complete invariant of an arbitrary $1$-type}

\subsection{Coproducts}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{coproduct} $\coprod_i\CC_i$ of a family of groupoids has objects the pairs
$(i,X)$ with $X\in\CC_i$, and morphisms $(i,X)\to(j,Y)$ the morphisms $X\to Y$ of $\CC_i$
when $i=j$, and none otherwise. It is again a groupoid.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Summands are separated]\label{lem:sep}
If $(i,X)\cong(j,Y)$ then $i=j$; moreover $\Aut((i,X))\cong\Aut(X)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:pi0coprod}
If each $\CC_i$ is connected at $c_i$ then $\pi_0(\coprod_i\CC_i)\cong I$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Every object $(i,X)$ is isomorphic to $(i,c_i)$ by connectedness, and distinct basepoints
are non-isomorphic by Lemma~\ref{lem:sep}; the map sending a class to its index is
therefore a bijection.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Assembling maps]\label{prop:assemble}
Let $e:I\to J$ and $F_i:\CC_i\to\DD_{e(i)}$. The assembled functor
$\coprod_iF_i$ is faithful if each $F_i$ is; full if each $F_i$ is full and $e$ is
injective; essentially surjective if each $F_i$ is and $e$ is surjective.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Gluing]\label{thm:glue}
A bijection $e:I\cong J$ together with equivalences $\CC_i\simeq\DD_{e(i)}$ yields an
equivalence $\coprod_i\CC_i\simeq\coprod_j\DD_j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of coproducts]\label{thm:coprod}
For families $(\CC_i)_{i\in I}$, $(\DD_j)_{j\in J}$ connected at $c_i$, $d_j$:
\[
\coprod_i\CC_i\simeq\coprod_j\DD_j
\iff \exists\,e:I\cong J\ \text{with}\ \Aut(c_i)\cong\Aut(d_{e(i)})\ \forall i.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Corollary~\ref{cor:complete-connected} turns each isomorphism of vertex
groups into an equivalence $\CC_i\simeq\DD_{e(i)}$; glue with Theorem~\ref{thm:glue}.
($\Rightarrow$) Given an equivalence $E$, let $e(i)$ be the index of the summand containing
$E(i,c_i)$; this is a bijection because $E$ induces a bijection on $\pi_0$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:pi0inv}), identified with $I\to J$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:pi0coprod}. For the groups,
$\Aut(c_i)\cong\Aut(i,c_i)\cong\Aut(E(i,c_i))\cong\Aut(e(i),d_{e(i)})\cong\Aut(d_{e(i)})$,
using Lemma~\ref{lem:sep} twice, Theorem~\ref{thm:whconv} in the middle, and connectedness
of $\DD_{e(i)}$ to move the basepoint.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Decomposition}

\begin{definition}
For $p\in\pi_0(\CC)$, let $\CC_p$ be the full subgroupoid on the objects of class $p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Each $\CC_p$ is connected, and its vertex group at an object is the automorphism group of
that object computed in $\CC$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Decomposition into components]\label{thm:decomp}
For every groupoid $\CC$, the assembly functor
$\coprod_{p\in\pi_0(\CC)}\CC_p\to\CC$, $(p,X)\mapsto X$, is an equivalence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Faithful: morphisms of the coproduct are morphisms of $\CC$. Full: a morphism $X\to Y$ in
$\CC$ forces $X,Y$ into the same component, so it already lies in the corresponding
summand. Essentially surjective: $X$ is the image of $([X],X)$ on the nose.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete invariant for arbitrary $1$-types]\label{thm:main}
Two groupoids $\CC,\DD$ are equivalent if and only if there is a bijection
$e:\pi_0(\CC)\cong\pi_0(\DD)$ such that for every component $p$ the fundamental groups of
the corresponding components are isomorphic, $\Aut(X_p)\cong\Aut(Y_{e(p)})$, where $X_p$,
$Y_q$ are any chosen representatives.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:decomp}, $\CC\simeq\coprod_p\CC_p$ and $\DD\simeq\coprod_q\DD_q$ with
connected summands; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:coprod}. Representatives may be changed freely
because a connected groupoid has isomorphic vertex groups at all objects.
\end{proof}

Thus the complete invariant of a homotopy $1$-type is the pair
$\big(\pi_0(X),(\pi_1(X,x_p))_{p\in\pi_0(X)}\big)$ up to bijection of the index set and
isomorphism of the groups --- the multiset of fundamental groups of the components. For a
connected $1$-type this is the single group $\pi_1$; for a totally disconnected one it is
the cardinality of $\pi_0$; and Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} is exactly the case of matching
(trivial) groups and non-matching component sets.

\section{Algorithms}

For finite groups every statement above becomes a finite computation.

\subsection{Enumerating homomorphisms}
A homomorphism is determined by the images of a generating set $S\subseteq G$, but not every
tuple of images extends. The \emph{generator-lifting} algorithm iterates over all
$|H|^{|S|}$ candidate assignments; for each it performs a breadth-first traversal of the
Cayley graph of $G$ with respect to $S$, propagating $\hat\varphi(g\cdot s)=\hat\varphi(g)
\hat\varphi(s)$ and rejecting as soon as a vertex is reached twice with conflicting values.
Survivors are verified on the full multiplication table. The cost is
$O(|H|^{|S|}\cdot|G|\cdot|S|)$.

\subsection{Homotopy classes of maps}
Form the conjugation orbits of $\Hom(G,H)$: repeatedly take an unclassified $\varphi$ and
mark $\{u\varphi(-)u^{-1}:u\in H\}$. The cost is $O(|\Hom(G,H)|\cdot|H|\cdot|G|)$.

\subsection{Verifying the counting theorem}
For each orbit representative compute $C_H(\varphi(G))$ by scanning $H$ for elements
commuting with all images, and compare $|H|/|C_H(\varphi(G))|$ with the orbit size
(Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre}) and the sum of orbit sizes with $|\Hom(G,H)|$
(Corollary~\ref{cor:count}).

\subsection{Deciding homotopy equivalence}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:main} a finite $1$-type is encoded by the multiset of isomorphism types
of the fundamental groups of its components; two are homotopy equivalent iff these
multisets agree, decided by sorting canonical invariants and comparing.

\section{Discussion}

\textbf{An equivalence of theories, not a mere invariant.} The classification is complete at
three levels: objects (Corollary~\ref{cor:complete-connected}), morphisms
(Corollary~\ref{cor:class}), and homotopies (Theorem~\ref{thm:homotopy}), and it is
compatible with composition (Theorem~\ref{thm:funct}). This is the precise sense in which
``connected homotopy $1$-types $=$ groups''.

\textbf{Basepoints and conjugacy are the same phenomenon.} The conjugation in
Theorem~\ref{thm:funct}(2) is the fingerprint of unbasedness; in the based setting the
classification would be a bijection with $\Hom(G,H)$ itself, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:fibre} measures the price of forgetting basepoints as the index of a
centralizer.

\textbf{The counterexample is optimal.} Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} defeats the whole homotopy
tower, showing that connectivity hypotheses in Whitehead-type theorems are not a technical
convenience. It also identifies the repair, completed by Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.

\textbf{Everything is computable.} Because the invariant is a multiset of groups and the
classification of maps is a set of conjugacy classes of homomorphisms, all statements become
finite computations for finite groups --- an unusual luxury in homotopy theory, and a direct
consequence of completeness.

\textbf{Scope.} For a general space the fundamental groupoid computes only the
$1$-truncation; sphere versus point remains the standard reminder that outside the
aspherical world the residue is strictly weaker than homotopy equivalence.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Bicategorical refinement.} Functoriality is established at the level of
homomorphisms and conjugacy classes; the full statement --- an equivalence of bicategories
between connected pointed $1$-types with maps and homotopies, and groups with homomorphisms
and conjugations --- requires coherence data, whose formulation and proof is the natural
next step.

\textbf{Beyond $1$-types.} For $2$-types the invariant becomes a crossed module: $\pi_1$,
$\pi_2$, the action of the former on the latter, and a $k$-invariant in
$H^3(\pi_1;\pi_2)$. The pattern here --- realization, homotopies-are-conjugations,
Whitehead, sharpness, decomposition --- is a template for that setting.

\textbf{Non-connected targets.} Corollary~\ref{cor:class} assumes both sides connected; for
a non-connected target the set of homotopy classes should decompose over target components,
and combining this with Theorem~\ref{thm:main} would compute $[\CC,\DD]$ in general.

\textbf{Enumerative consequences.} Corollary~\ref{cor:count} expresses $|\Hom(G,H)|$ as a
sum of centralizer indices; for $G$ cyclic this counts conjugacy classes of elements of
order dividing $|G|$, linking the homotopy-theoretic count to classical counting theorems
of Frobenius type.

\textbf{Infinite and topological groups.} The arguments use only group structure and choice
of paths, so they apply to arbitrary groups; extending them to topological groupoids, where
conjugacy classes acquire a topology and the classification becomes a statement about
mapping spaces, is a further direction.

\end{document}

